You are an expert judge of technical explainers. Compare two web pages, A and B. Both were made for the same request:

"__REQUEST__"

Files in the current directory:
- A/page.html and B/page.html: the full source of each page. Each is one self-contained HTML file.

There are no screenshots. You must use each page yourself, like a reader, in a real headless Chrome. Use this driver:

  node __DRIVER__

Run it with no arguments first and read its usage. It loads a page, runs a list of actions (click, key, move, drag, wait, eval, shot) and prints the page's console errors. Useful options: --width 1440 or --width 390 (phone), --height, --reduced (reduced motion), --theme dark|light. Put long action lists in a JSON file and pass it with --actions. Save your screenshots in shots-A/ and shots-B/, and open every screenshot you take with view_file. A screenshot you do not open does not count.

Steps:
1. Read both HTML files, including the scripts, so you know every control each page has.
2. Use page A and then page B as a reader would. On each page, at least:
   - open it at 1440 px and look at the first view;
   - press every main control (play, next, step, scene buttons, presets) and look at the result;
   - use each live input with your own value (type a key or a number, click a cell, drag a slider), then look at whether the drawing, the numbers and the text all follow your value;
   - try the keyboard (Tab, arrows, Enter);
   - open it at 390 px and look at it;
   - open it once with --reduced.
   Spend the same effort on both pages.
3. Check the technical claims and the numbers you saw on screen. Report every factual error, and every place where the drawing, the math and the text disagree.
4. Score each page from 1 to 10 on each criterion below. Use the whole scale. A 10 means nothing could be better.

Criteria:
- accuracy: the technical claims and numbers are correct.
- core_idea_clarity: after one viewing, a newcomer could explain the core idea in their own words.
- visual_explanation: the visuals show the real mechanism, not only text next to pictures.
- interactivity: the reader can explore, step and change things, and the motion helps understanding.
- request_coverage: how fully the page answers every part of the request.
- depth: useful insight beyond the basics.
- visual_design: polish, layout, typography, colour use and consistency.
- mobile_and_accessibility: works at phone width, keyboard use, readable text, reduced motion.

For each page, list in "interactions_tried" what you did (one short line each), and write "interaction_feedback": what worked, what broke or confused you, and what a reader would want to do but cannot.

Then pick an overall winner: "A", "B" or "tie". Judge which page better serves a reader who asked this request. Do not prefer a page because it is longer, and do not prefer A or B because of its position. Give your reasons in a few short sentences, and name the single biggest strength and the single biggest weakness of each page.

In "screenshots_viewed", list every screenshot file you took and opened.
